WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.840
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.000
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.360
Where do you go?

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.720
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.760
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.280
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.800
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.160
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.360
Today we take a close
look at a multi-faceted

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.640
and often misunderstood
root vegetable.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.720
And we start with a little trick about
lowering their glycemic impact.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.000
If you systematically pull
together all the best studies

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.720
on potato consumption
and chronic disease risk,

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.240
an association is
found for the risk

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.480
of type 2 diabetes
and hypertension.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.200
Yeah, but that was
for French fries.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.160
Consumption of boiled, baked,
or mashed potatoes was not

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.320
associated with the risk
of high blood pressure,

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.520
but there was still a
pesky link with diabetes.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.680
Overall, potato consumption
is not related to the risk

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.480
for many chronic diseases,
but boiled potatoes

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.840
could potentially pose a small
increase in risk for diabetes.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.880
That’s one of the reasons
some question whether they should

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.600
be counted as vegetables
when you’re trying to reach

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.320
your recommended daily servings
of fruits and vegetables.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.840
If you look at other whole
plant foods—nuts, vegetables,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.360
fruits, and legumes (which
are beans, split peas,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.320
chickpeas, and lentils)—
they’re associated with

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.400
living a longer life.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.000
Significantly less risk
of dying from cancer,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.360
dying from cardiovascular
diseases like heart attacks,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.840
and 25 percent less
chance of dying prematurely

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.240
from all causes put together.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.880
But no such protection
is gained from potatoes

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.680
for cancer, heart disease,
or overall mortality.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.560
So, the fact that potatoes
don’t seem to affect mortality

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.280
can be seen as a downside.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.280
Now, it’s not like meat,
which may actually

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.680
actively shorten your
life, but there may be

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.080
an opportunity cost to
eating white potatoes,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.480
since every bite of a
potato is a lost opportunity

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.480
to put something even
healthier in your mouth—

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.960
something that may
actively make you live longer.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.320
So, potatoes are kind
of a double-edge sword.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.320
The reason that potato
consumption may just have

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.720
a neutral impact on
mortality risk is that

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.120
all the fiber, vitamin C, and
potassium in white potatoes

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.720
might be counterbalanced
by the detrimental effects

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.240
of their high glycemic index.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.600
Not only are high glycemic
impact diets robustly

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.760
associated with
developing type 2 diabetes,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.200
but current evidence suggests that

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.520
this relationship
is cause-and-effect.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.040
A front group for the
potato industry called

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.160
the Alliance for Potato
Research and Education

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.120
funded a study that found
that intake of non-fried potato

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.280
does not affect blood
sugar markers,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.800
but that’s compared with
the likes of Wonder Bread;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.040
so, that isn’t really
saying very much.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.960
Foods with a glycemic
index (GI) above 70

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.880
are classiﬁed as high-GI foods,
high glycemic index foods,

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.880
and those lower than
55 are low-GI foods.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.760
Pure sugar water, for example,
is often standardized at 100.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.387
White bread and white
potatoes are way up there

00:03:09.387 --> 00:03:11.440
as high glycemic index foods.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.600
When you compare them to an
intact grain, like barley groats

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.440
(also known as pot barley),
a super-low GI food,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.600
you can see how refined grains
and potatoes are simply no match.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.440
Is there any way we can have
our potatoes and eat them too,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.080
by somehow lowering
their glycemic index?

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.680
Well, if you boil potatoes
and then put them

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.120
in the fridge to cool, some
of the starch crystallizes

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.120
into a form that can no
longer be broken down

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.600
by the starch-munching
enzymes in your gut.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.720
However, the amounts of this
so-called resistant starch

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.520
that are formed are relatively
small, making it difﬁcult

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.600
to recommend cold potatoes
as the solution.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.080
But when put to the test, you
actually see a dramatic drop

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.600
in glycemic index in cold
versus hot potatoes.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.280
So, by consuming potatoes
as potato salad, for instance,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.240
you can get nearly a 40 percent
lower glycemic impact.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.920
The chilling effect might,
therefore, also slow the rate

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.560
at which the starch is
broken down and absorbed.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.320
So, individuals wishing to
minimize dietary glycemic index

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.160
may be advised
to precook potatoes

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.880
and consume them
cold or reheated.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.280
The downside of eating potatoes
cold is that they might not

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.160
be as satiating as
eating hot potatoes.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.880
But you may get the best
of both worlds by cooling them

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.520
then reheating them, which
is exactly what was done

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.800
in that famous study I profiled
in my book How Not to Diet.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.920
The single most satiating
food out of the dozens tested

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.760
was boiled then cooled
then reheated potatoes.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.440
There’s actually an appetite-
suppressing protein in potatoes

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.600
called potato
protease inhibitor II,

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.800
but the way you prepare your
potatoes makes a difference.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.840
Both boiled and mashed
potatoes are significantly

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.000
more satiating
than French fries.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.880
That was for fried
French fries, though.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.400
What about baked French fries?

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.320
Folks had a big drop in
appetite after eating

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.520
boiled mashed potatoes, compared
to white rice or white pasta,

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.480
which is right where
fried French fries were stuck,

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.280
as well as baked French fries.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.880
So, though they may be your BFF,
they’re not very satiating.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.920
In our next story, broccoli,
vinegar, and lemon juice

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.200
are put to the test to blunt the
glycemic index of white potatoes.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.200
White potatoes have a high glycemic
index, and consumption

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.120
of high glycemic impact foods may
increase the risk of diabetes.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.680
Normally after a meal, we’d
like our blood sugars

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.880
to just gently, naturally
rise and fall.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.840
But with high glycemic foods
like potatoes, you get an

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.160
exaggerated blood sugar spike
which leads your body

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.280
to over-compensate with insulin
forcing your blood sugars

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.320
lower than when you started, which
results in negative metabolic

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.000
consequences such as a rise in
triglyceride fats in the blood.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.280
However, potatoes are a good
source of potassium, vitamin C,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.800
and polyphenols, which may
counterbalance the glycemic impact.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.520
This may explain why potatoes
appear to have a neutral effect

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.320
when it comes to lifespan,
unlike other whole plant foods,

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.600
that have been associated
with actively living longer.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.320
In my last video, I detailed
my nip-and-nuke method,

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.280
where the act of chilling potatoes
can dramatically lower their

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.320
glycemic index, even if you then
reheat them in a microwave.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.480
How else might we reduce the
glycemic impact of white potatoes?

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.360
The answer is the same
way you make anything better

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.480
in your nutritional
life—add broccoli.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.360
The co-consumption of two
servings of cooked broccoli

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.520
with your mashed potatoes
would certainly do it,

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.720
immediately cutting the insulin
demand by nearly 40 percent.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.760
In contrast, adding chicken
breast makes things worse

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.360
and adding tuna fish makes
things even worse still,

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.400
nearly doubling the amount of
insulin your body has to pump out.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.680
Why does plant protein
make things better,

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.560
but animal protein
make things worse?

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.360
Because decreased consumption
of branched-chain amino acids

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.400
improves metabolic health.
I cover this in my book

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.600
How Not to Diet as well as
my video on the topic.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.840
Speaking of How Not to Diet,
remember the section on vinegar?

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.742
Here are the blood sugar and
insulin spikes someone with

00:07:10.742 --> 00:07:12.960
prediabetes can get
from eating a bagel.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.400
Eat that same bagel with
a tablespoon or so

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.360
of apple cider vinegar diluted in
about a quarter cup of water,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.680
though, the impact is
significantly less.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.800
Does it work for potatoes too?

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:28.480
Simply chilling potatoes may
cut down on the blood sugar

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:32.160
and insulin spikes, but to
get significant drops in both,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.640
you just have to add about
a tablespoon of vinegar

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.800
to drop levels by
30 to 40 percent.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.760
And that was just plain
white distilled vinegar.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:45.040
Is it the vinegar itself, or
would any acidy condiment do?

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:49.760
In a test tube, lemon juice
appeared to have a remarkable

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:53.120
starch-blocking effect, but
you can’t know if it works

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.480
in people, until you...
put it to the test.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:00.720
And indeed, lemon juice reduces
the glycemic responses to bread.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.960
And not just by a little,
but by like 30 percent.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.840
Now, the subjects were drinking
a half cup of lemon juice,

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.720
but that makes it even more
remarkable that it helped because

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.120
that added an extra half
teaspoon of sugar,

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.840
and yet they still had a
better blood sugar response.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.320
Vinegar is more potent, though.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.480
Just one to two tablespoons a
day of vinegar diluted in water

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.280
can significantly improve
both short- and long-term

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.720
blood sugar control in diabetics,
which is why clinicians

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.040
may want to incorporate
vinegar consumption as part

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.640
of their dietary advice for
patients with diabetes.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.800
Finally today, are yellow-
fleshed potatoes

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.160
healthier than white?
Let’s find out.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.360
The high glycemic impact of
potatoes may increase the risk

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.240
of type 2 diabetes, perhaps by
chronically overstimulating

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:50.320
the insulin-producing
cells in the pancreas.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.360
In my last two videos I
explained how you can decrease

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.480
the glycemic impact of white
potatoes by eating them cold,

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.520
or chilling then reheating
them, or adding broccoli,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.640
lemon juice, or vinegar.
What else can we do?

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.880
Well, if you remember, I had
a video a few years back

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.320
that showed how the pigments
in brightly colored berries

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.400
can act as starch blockers.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.640
So, if you’re going to eat
a high glycemic food,

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:16.240
you may be able to moderate its
impact by, spreading raspberries

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.880
on your toast, for example,
or adding strawberries

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.320
to your cornflakes, or sprinkling
blueberries in your pancakes.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.560
No, I’m not saying you have
to put blackberries in your

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.760
baked potato, but given
that the natural color compounds

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.760
in fruits can slow
down starch digestion,

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.160
what about pigmented potatoes.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.440
Even just yellow potatoes like
Yukon gold may be preferable

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:39.360
to white, but the best
may be purple potatoes:

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.480
not just purple-skinned potatoes
but purple-fleshed potatoes.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.240
If you’ve never seen purple
potatoes, they are remarkable;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.040
they have almost
a neon blue glow.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.480
And not only do they look
cool, but purple potatoes

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.120
cause less of an insulin and
blood sugar spike compared

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.760
to even the yellow-fleshed
potatoes, suggesting that

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.480
switching from yellow or
white to purple could have

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.720
a large potential
public health benefit.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.240
How do we know it’s
the pigments themselves

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.560
that are responsible
rather than other differences

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:10.400
between the different potato varieties?

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:14.640
The researchers tried adding a
control comprised of berries in a

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:18.560
potato starch jelly, but that would
seem to add even more variables.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.200
In a test tube, extracts of
purple- and red-fleshed potatoes

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:27.120
can act as starch blockers;
so hey, if you can extract

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.680
and purify out the purple-
potato-pigments—

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:31.360
say that five times fast—

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.320
you could remove any other
effects of the different

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.840
potato varieties by adding the
purple pigments to yellow potatoes.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.520
And low and behold, compared
to just plain yellow potatoes,

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.400
you can get a suppression of
the blood sugar and insulin spikes.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:48.800
This way you don’t get that
overshoot reaction where your

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.280
blood sugars can actually
drop below fasting

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.640
like you might get otherwise;
instead, you get the gentler

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:58.080
up and down in blood sugars you’d
expect from a lower glycemic food.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:02.400
The authors suggest purple
potato extracts could be produced

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.520
and made into supplements
or fancy functional foods,

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.200
but these health-promoting
compounds may be more cost-effectively

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.160
received from consuming
purple potatoes themselves.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.800
The Potato Association of
America likes to paint potatoes

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:20.000
as an anti-inflammatory food,
but what they don’t tell you

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:23.840
is that this benefit may be
limited to pigmented potatoes.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.240
If you randomize people to eat
a small white potato every day

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.000
for six weeks versus a yellow-
or purple-fleshed potato,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.040
the purple potato group achieved
significantly lower levels

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.920
of inflammation compared
to the white potato group,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.720
measuring both c-reactive
protein and interleukin-6.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.520
Pigmented potato consumption
also alters oxidative stress.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:46.080
Within hours of eating a large
purple potato, you get a nice

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.960
60 percent bump in the antioxidant
power of your bloodstream,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.560
and this translates into
less free radical DNA damage.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:56.320
If you compare the antioxidant
activity of white potatoes,

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:59.600
yellow potatoes, and purple
potatoes, Yukon gold has about

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:01.600
twice the antioxidant power of white,

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:04.560
but purple has twenty
times the antioxidants.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:06.960
Which is comparable to what
you might see in berries.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:10.480
Half of purple potato has
about the same polyphenol

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.960
antioxidant complement as
half cup of blueberries.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.160
Purple potatoes can increase
the antioxidant capacity

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.120
of our blood stream, whereas
straight white potato starch

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.680
can act as a pro-oxidant
and decrease it.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:24.640
Eat a purple potato, and over
the next eight hours the

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.520
antioxidant capacity of
your blood stream goes up.

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:31.280
In contrast, if you eat white
potato starch devoid of any pigment,

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.200
you can end up worse
off than where you started.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.760
Okay, but does this translate
into different physiological effects?

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.680
Yes indeed. Have people eat
either purple potatoes or

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.480
white potatoes for two weeks,
and the purple potatoes

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:48.720
improved a measure of
arterial stiffness,

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:51.120
whereas the white potato did not.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.920
And this translates into
a drop in blood pressure,

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:56.560
even in those already taking
high blood pressure drugs,

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:58.720
suggesting purple
potatoes are an effective

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.240
blood pressure lowering agent.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.840
But what about the toxic
glycoalkaloid compounds in potatoes?

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.800
The toxic human dose starts
around 2-5 mg/kg of body weight,

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:13.760
and the lethal dose is
not too far behind.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.760
But the average amount of total
glycoalkaloids in most potatoes,

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:22.480
however, is less than 100 mg/kg;
so, at the average American

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:25.440
weight of 180 pounds,
a toxic dose is like

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.680
four to nine pounds of potatoes.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.000
What happens when you approach that?

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.120
It’s possible: nausea,
vomiting, or diarrhea

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.200
that could easily be
confused with something like

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:37.120
gastroenteritis or food poisoning.

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.520
Some people can start to get sick

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:45.200
at just 1.25 mg/kg of
body weight, or even 1 mg/kg.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.680
That would only be about
two pounds of potatoes

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.520
at the average American weight.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:52.080
It’s also possible they
could start accumulating

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.760
if you eat them day
in and day out.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:57.120
But what about those people
who go on a fad potato diet

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:58.720
and eat three or
four pounds a day?

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:03.120
They can do that without risking
getting sick only if they peel

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:07.324
their potatoes, which removes
nearly all the glycoalkaloids.

